# =============================================================================
# Docker build-context exclusions.
#
# Mirrors .gitignore's own reasoning almost exactly (see that file's
# header comment): everything excluded below is either re-created
# automatically by this project's own bootstrap/download logic on first
# use, or is genuinely local/host-only state that must never be baked
# into an image. Keeping the build context small also makes `docker
# build` itself faster -- geper/plugin_model_cache/ alone is ~3.1GB on a
# machine that has already run GEPER once; sending that to the Docker
# daemon on every build (even though a COPY would never reference it)
# would be pure waste.
# =============================================================================

# --- Version control / editor / OS cruft ------------------------------------
.git/
.gitignore
.claude/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# --- Python ------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# --- AI model weights / reference data (auto-downloaded on first use;
#     baked into the image only where explicitly justified in the
#     Dockerfile -- e.g. geper/hyena-dna/'s SOURCE, not its weights) ---------
# MEASURED 2026-09-10 in geper:kelly-master-d249a20: the line below USED TO
# READ `geper/plugin_model_cache/` and the cache it was written for lives at
# the REPOSITORY ROOT, so 2.6 GB of borzoi/enformer/spliceformer weights were
# reaching /app/plugin_model_cache in every build. A .dockerignore rule that
# matches nothing fails SILENTLY -- the build succeeds and the payload is
# simply there. Both paths are listed because both have existed.
plugin_model_cache/
geper/plugin_model_cache/
geper/hyena-dna/
geper/checkpoints/

# --- Evidence-source local datasets / bulk downloads (auto-bootstrapped) ----
geper/model_cache/

# --- Run output / logs --------------------------------------------------------
geper/geper_output/
geper/logs/
# `*.log` ALONE MATCHES ONLY THE CONTEXT ROOT. MEASURED 2026-09-10: with only
# that rule, /app/logs/geper.log shipped inside the image -- 2.3 MB and 18,414
# lines of a real session. A runtime log is a DISCLOSURE question before it is
# a size one: it carries paths, sample ids and whatever was being run.
*.log
**/*.log

# --- Docker-related files themselves (no reason for the image to contain
#     its own Dockerfile/compose file) ----------------------------------------
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore

# --- Local secrets / overrides (must never be baked into an image) ----------
.env
.env.*
geper/config.yaml
!geper/config.yaml.example

# --- Offline model-cache seed ------------------------------------------
# NOT an optimisation, and do not remove it to "let the image carry the
# models". It cannot: the seed's HuggingFace layout contains five
# relative symlinks (models--facebook--esm2_t33_650M_UR50D/snapshots/
# <sha>/{config.json,model.safetensors,special_tokens_map.json,
# tokenizer_config.json,vocab.txt} -> ../../blobs/<sha>), and BuildKit
# refuses to load a context containing them:
#
#     ERROR: invalid file request model_cache_seed/models--facebook--
#     esm2_t33_650M_UR50D/snapshots/08e4846e.../config.json
#
# Measured twice, on 2026-09-09, by two agents on two different
# Dockerfiles. Without this line the repository does not build AT ALL
# from a checkout that has the seed on disk -- and the error names a
# model cache rather than anything to do with the change being made, so
# whoever hits it searches their own diff and finds nothing.
#
# The seed reaches a container by RUNTIME BIND MOUNT, never through the
# build context -- see DOCKER.md. Excluding it therefore costs a
# capability that has never existed.
model_cache_seed/
model_cache_seed.tar.gz

# Note: geper/testdata/, geper/testdata_bench/, and the repo-root
# test_data/ are intentionally NOT excluded here (same reasoning
# .gitignore documents) -- they're tiny, purpose-built fixtures, not the
# large re-fetchable caches this file targets, and having them in the
# image is useful for exactly the kind of "does this image actually
# work" smoke test this task's Colab verification will run.
